<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-continuity-of-rational-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Continuity of rational functions
  </title>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove using one or more characterizations of continuity that the real-valued function <m> f \colon \R \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = x </m> is continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> V \subseteq \R </m> be an open subset of the real line <m> \R </m>, and note that
        
        <md>
          f^{ -1 } \of{ V } = \setmid{ x \in \R }{ f \of{ x } \in V } = \setmid{ x \in \R }{ x \in V } = V
        </md>
        
        is open in <m> \R </m>. Since the pre-image of any open subset of <m> \R </m> is open in <m> \R </m>, <m> f </m> is continuous.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Argue that any polynomial function is continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        A polynomial function is by definition a linear combination of finite products of <m> f </m> with itself, and so is continuous by (a) above and <xref ref="proposition-vector-arithmetic-of-continuous-maps"/>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Argue that any rational function is continuous on its implicit domain.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        A rational function is by definition a fraction of polynomial functions, and so is continuous by (b) above and <xref ref="corollary-arithmetic-of-continuous-functions"/>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
